            "text": "Some mornings arrive like lightly struck bells, their tones trembling through the rooms of memory before I have even opened my eyes. I lie there listening, pretending that sound alone can keep the world from rushing in. Far outside, garbage trucks brake in the fog, gulls negotiate in a dialect of hunger, and somewhere a dog insists on being heard. It amazes me that such ordinary clamor does not cancel the subtle bell still ringing inside my skull. Instead the two weave together\u2014a loom of grit and grace, steel teeth pulling threads I won\u2019t fully recognize until much later.  \n\nBy the time coffee clouds its black with a swirl of oat milk, I\u2019m already cataloguing the day\u2019s improbable details. The first spiderweb gleaming on the porch rail, the neighbor\u2019s child reciting new multiplication tables to the rhythm of the swing set, the smell of rain before it commits to falling. I try to inventory these crumbs the way a traveler might pocket pebbles from each coastline, proof that a wandering body still touched ground.  \n\nBut memory is always two stories tall: the one you enter and the one you must leap from when fire climbs the stairs. I\u2019ve leapt often, usually with only a sentence clamped between my teeth, hoping it would open into a parachute before impact. Sometimes it did, flowering into a poem or an apology or a joke so perfectly timed it rescued everyone at the table. Other times I landed hard, words folding like broken wings, letting the ground knock the silence out of me.  \n\nI wonder, now, how many versions of myself are still suspended in midair somewhere, paused between peril and possibility because I never finished the thought. Does a sentence abandoned emit its own frequency, a small persistent hum that unsettles the furniture of the brain? I\u2019d like to believe so; it would explain the odd vibrations that sometimes rattle me awake just before dawn.  \n\nDawn, for its part, keeps its promises quietly. It never guarantees grandeur, only illumination, and even that is leased, not owned. In the kitchen, color tiptoes back into the oranges, the knives remember their shine, a jar of cumin becomes the desert it once wandered. The ordinary is forever rehearsing its miracles; our role is mostly to notice the curtain lifting.  \n\nNoticing, however, is labor, and labor has enemies: hurry, fatigue, the relentless lure of glowing rectangles. I am as susceptible as anyone, caught scrolling while tea goes cold, trading breath for bright useless sparks. Whole mornings have slipped through that shimmering net; when I pull them back they are skeletal, contexts chewed away.  \n\nSo I practice small saboteur acts against distraction: airplane mode, handwritten lists, the sudden pilgrimage to the window where real weather debates itself. I trace clouds like new consonants, guess their sounds, let the lungs translate. Sometimes a crow will land on the power line, balancing the sky on a single black syllable. We stare at each other, conspirators briefly freed from our respective alphabets.  \n\nI used to think the purpose of writing was to nail reality to the page, to stop it squirming away. Now I\u2019m convinced it is closer to the art of slacklining: you step out over absence, trust tension, and accept sway as implicit. The line sings beneath each foot, reminding you that balance is a verb, not a state. If you reach the other side, wonderful; if not, the fall is still sky.  \n\nSky has its own archives, though we rarely request them. At night, I sometimes lean against the railing and imagine each star as an unopened file\u2014the light of what happened long ago, couriered through dark bureaucracy. I wonder which permissions are required to read those documents. Perhaps patience is the stamp, wonder the inkpad. Or perhaps we already read them whenever we dream of someone who can no longer answer the phone.  \n\nThe phone itself is a relic that refuses extinction. I keep one old landline tucked in a drawer, its coiled cord sleeping like a snail that believes in thunder. Some part of me trusts that if storms ever knock us back to candles, that phone will wake and speak dial tone like an incantation. I don\u2019t know whom I would call; maybe the past keeps an operator on duty.  \n\nSpeaking of duty, the kettle whistles again, reminding me that water too seeks purpose. Left alone it would rather become a mirror or a river, but I conscript it into steam and sip its surrender. In doing so I inherit the warmth of a thousand distant suns, because even the glacier once drank starlight.  \n\nThis thought comforts me when headlines clamor that everything is spiraling beyond salvage. Spirals are not always descents; they can be ascents viewed from the wrong balcony. If history is a staircase, perhaps we are only dizzy, not doomed. And dizziness passes, especially when companions steady us with stories.  \n\nSo let me offer one more before my allotted words evaporate. There was a town on the coast where every window contained a seashell instead of a candle. They said at night the shells sang back the tide, guiding fishermen home even in fog thick enough to smother compass needles. But one season the harvest of shells failed, and darkness grew feral. The townspeople could have chosen lamps, but instead they learned by heart the pitch of each returning boat\u2019s engine and took shifts on the pier, chanting those tones into the vast night. Months later, when shells resurfaced, no one bothered to collect them; they had discovered their own throats were lanterns.  \n\nI carry that story for the days my voice feels matchstick-thin, a reminder that sound, like light, can grow muscular with use. So these words are a small exercise, a morning run along the margin of silence, and you, reader, are the unexpected companion who kept pace. Thank you for sharing the breath of this sentence, whose echo will keep ringing even after you close the page. Farewell now."
